 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeRu2BDJwGA/",
 "title": "Handling a Postponed Product Launch Crisis",
 "topic": "supply chain",
 "summary": "This video shows a scenario where a product launch is postponed, causing panic about heavy financial losses from idle raw materials and production capacity. The main lesson is to systematically assess what is actually at risk, utilize existing shelf life, repurpose raw materials for other products, and reallocate remaining capacity to best-selling items instead of panicking or canceling production entirely.",
 "key_points": [
  "A product launch delay can trigger panic about losing capital tied up in raw materials and production capacity.",
  "Check the shelf life of the raw materials (e.g., 4 months) and see if the launch date still falls within that window.",
  "Assess how much of the material is actually needed for the launch; marketing often expects only half of the total quantity.",
  "Repurpose a portion of the raw materials (e.g., about 30%) for other products that can use them immediately.",
  "Do not cancel production completely; instead, produce only the necessary quantity needed for the launch using the materials at risk first.",
  "Use the remaining production slots for an existing, selling product that actually needs the capacity."
 ],
 "content": "POV: YOUR TOXIC BBOSS CALLS YOU AT 6PM ON FRIDAY EVENING. Heart Rate :80 Bpm. Hello sir, happy Friday. Hey, I want you to work on the weekend. What? Why? I'm sorry, but we have a very big problem. I think we are going to lose 50 crores. Really? How? What happened? You know the new Lakme lipstick we were launching? It has been postponed by two months by the marketing team. Okay. And we have already scheduled production for tomorrow. Why is that 50 crore problem? We already have 40 crores of raw materials that we ordered in the factory sitting idle, and we have blocked another 10 crores of production capacity for tomorrow. Got it. Got it. That makes sense. And since launch is postponed, most of that money is gone. Okay, so have a question, what's the shelf life of the raw materials right now? Four months. And how much of the lipstick do we actually need for the launch? Marketing only expects half of that quantity. How much of the raw materials can we use for other products then? About 30%. So the six-week delay isn't actually the issue then? What do you mean? We still have enough shelf life to use the material at the launch date, right? Fine, but uh we still have 40 crores sitting there. Yeah, agreed, but uh I wouldn't cancel production completely though in the situation. What would you do? I would produce the quantity we need for the launch, use the raw material that's at risk first, and repurpose whatever capacity and material we can for other products that can probably use this raw material and be produced right now. And the remaining production slot? Let's use it for an existing product that's actually selling and needs the capacity. Okay, I'm so relieved. So how much of that 50 crore are we actually going to lose now? You're welcome and maybe none. The first thing I would do is separate what is actually at risk from what is simply. Text on screen: Send this video to your friends who have experienced the same! #interview #supplychain #Career #boss",
